Narrator: [Scene 5: Thursday Afternoon - Showing Up] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Convention_Center_Atrium.jpg] [BACKGROUND: CID Building / Convention Center Atrium, 2:15 p.m. Polished glass reflections under a clearing, overcast sky. Students in blazers and faculty with clipboards stream into the grand atrium for the Undergraduate Research Symposium.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The lively, echoing buzz of the convention atrium, punctuated by polite bursts of applause from the presentation halls.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, middle] The calibration array is patched -- inelegantly, at 3:30 a.m., with a comment block in the code that literally reads `// Maya Sterling saved this struct at 1:15 AM, do not touch` -- but it is 100% functional.
Narrator: The twenty-six-page discussion section was submitted at 1:00 p.m., four hours past Dr. Okoye's 9:00 a.m. target, accompanied by a three-sentence, unvarnished email explaining the struct offset bug that Jules rewrote exactly zero times before hitting Send. Dr. Okoye's reply ten minutes ago was two words: *Received. Sleep.*
Narrator: Now, standing outside the glass double doors of the CID Atrium at 2:15 p.m., Jules feels the weird, weightless sensation of a runner who has finally crossed a finish line and doesn't know what to do with his legs.
Narrator: Nora's symposium presentation for the Callahan Prize is in fifteen minutes. Back in his dorm room, however, his secondary terminal still has two non-critical memory leak warnings in the UI wrapper that he hasn't chased down before Monday's RTX demo.

? Narrator: Nora's symposium presentation is inside. The two compiler warnings are waiting in his dorm terminal. Which does Jules choose?
    > Skip the symposium. The warnings won't fix themselves. (Choose the machine)
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Stressed, middle] The old reflex wins. If an RTX engineer asks to see his live memory profiler on Monday and spots an unfreed heap allocation, he won't have an excuse.
        Narrator: Jules pulls out his phone, types a hurried text to Nora -- *Good luck today, stuck on a memory leak in the lab, rooting for you* -- and turns his back on the glass doors of the atrium.
        Narrator: [BG: Study_Room.jpg] [HIDE: all] [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, middle] He walks briskly back toward the engineering annex. The two compiler warnings take forty minutes to isolate and resolve. He spends the next twenty minutes sitting alone in a windowless lab bay, staring blankly at a green `[0 Warnings, 0 Errors]` checkmark, feeling utterly, echoing hollow inside.
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: Health & Boundaries -1, Burnout +1]
    > Go to the symposium. The warnings can wait fifteen minutes. (Show up for someone else)
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Neutral, left] [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, right] Jules takes a breath, silences the nagging voice in his head about heap allocations, and pushes open the heavy glass doors of the CID Atrium.
        Narrator: Three rows back from the center podium in Section B, standing out sharply among the pressed slacks and blazers in her clean jeans and work boots, is Maya. She's clutching a crinkly, plastic-wrapped bouquet of $4.99 Shell gas-station carnations dyed bright orange and pink.
        Maya_Smiling(right): (whispering as Jules slides into the folding chair beside her) Well, well. Look who made it out of the engineering bunker into natural sunlight. Don't judge the bouquet -- they smell like unleaded fuel and desperation, it was all the corner Mart had left.
        Jules_Smiling(left): (pulling his phone from his pocket, flipping it into landscape mode with the video camera primed at 4K) Second place, first place, or honorable mention, she's walking away with gas-station flowers and archival footage.
        Narrator: [BG: Symposium_1A.jpg] [SPRITE: Nora_Speaking, middle] At 2:30 sharp, Nora Vance steps up to the podium next to her glossy CRISPR-Cas12 biosensor poster. Her hands don't shake once. She talks openly and clearly about bloodstream infection detection windows, about her Graveyard Tab of eleven failed prototypes, and about the electrochemical breakthrough on try twelve. She is brilliant, unhurried, and genuinely in command of the entire atrium.
        Narrator: [BG: Convention_Center_Atrium.jpg] [SPRITE: Nora_Distressed, middle] When the judges finish deliberations at four o'clock and clip the shiny blue-and-silver Second Place Callahan Prize ribbon to her display board, the crowd applauds warmly -- and then Nora's phone buzzes twice in her hand.
        Narrator: Jules watches through his camera viewfinder as the light drains out of Nora's posture in real time: her mother texting sixty words about her brother's finance job offer and six dismissive words about her "poster thing," right alongside her girlfriend spacing on the presentation entirely.
        Narrator: Before the silence around the podium can swallow her, Maya steps up shoulder-to-shoulder beside Nora with the neon carnations, and Jules lowers his phone, stepping in on her other side to ask softly: *"Hey. Do you want to get out of here?"*
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: Vulnerability +1, Health & Boundaries +1, Burnout -1]

Narrator: [Scene 5 Continued: Thursday Early Evening - The Shared Plate] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: The_Mill_Patio_Dusk.jpg] [BACKGROUND: The Mill / Student Center covered patio, 5:30 p.m. String lights glow warmly overhead against the encroaching autumn dusk. A large pepperoni pizza box, three paper coffee cups, and a heaped metal basket of seasoned curly fries sit in the center of the round mesh table.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: Low indie guitar music drifting from the cafe speakers, distant campus chatter, and the gentle patter of light evening mist on the patio awning.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Smiling, left] [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, middle] [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, right] The three of them end up at the patio corner table as the late afternoon light fades into deep indigo. Nora's presentation poster tube rests dry against the brick wall beside her chair, the Second Place Callahan Prize ribbon pinned proudly to her hoodie pocket where Maya forced her to wear it, and the gas-station carnations sit in an empty plastic iced-coffee cup in the middle of the table like a centerpiece.
Narrator: [HIDE: all] [BG: Special_Image_1A.jpg] After Nora finally lets the airlock open -- admitting out loud that her family hasn't watched her finish a sentence in ten years and her girlfriend hasn't asked a real question since February -- Jules unlocks his phone and slides it across the metal table.
Narrator: On the screen is the full twelve-minute video of her talk, framed steady and clear, with Dr. Okafor beaming like a proud parent in the background.
Jules: For what it's worth, Vance, you have hard empirical proof of what you built today. I already backed it up to Google Drive. No matter what anyone else was busy doing at two-thirty, this happened.
Nora: (letting out a wet, surprised laugh, wiping the corner of her eye with her thumb) You backed it up to the cloud? You really are a menace, Rivera. Thank you.
Narrator: [BG: Special_Image_1B.jpg] Nora suddenly reaches across the table, snatches a crispy double-loop curly fry right out from under Jules's fingers, and pops it in her mouth with a triumphant grin.
Nora: That's the Finder's Fee. And as the newly crowned Second-Place Callahan Sovereign of the cafe, all curly fries within a two-foot radius belong to the crown. Om nom nom.
Jules: (holding up his hands in mock surrender) Unbelievable. Highway robbery under ambient cafe lighting. I'm filing a formal grievance with the National Science Foundation.
Maya: [BG: Special_Image_1C.jpg] (stealing the next longest fry and waving it like a conductor's baton) Case dismissed. Now, my turn to interrogate. Jules. Day four of the RTX Death March. Give us the status report. Did Dr. Okoye flay you alive for submitting at one p.m.?
Jules: (leaning back in the metal chair, his voice cracking with a wry, theatrical shrug) I survived by playing dead like the rats in the Student Center. When she walked by, I didn't blink for three minutes so she'd assume I was an interactive statue honoring student debt. But status report: the drone -- sorry, the IMU calibration array -- did not, in fact, kill me. Okoye read the bug note, accepted the late upload without a grade penalty, and ordered me to consume actual organic matter that didn't come in a powdered sachet.
Jules: Apparently, I have it on very good veterinary authority that the floor exists, and hitting it isn't fatal.
Maya: (grinning broadly, raising her coffee cup in a deadpan toast) Look at you, quoting me back to me. Put me in your acknowledgments section when you're rich and working for Google.
Jules: [BG: Special_Image_1D.jpg] You're already in the header comments of `kalman_bridge.h`. Your name is permanently compiled into the department repository.
Nora: (holding up her slice of pepperoni pizza like a shield, making little laser noises) Pew pew! Look at us. A cybernetic bunker commander, a cow whisperer, and a girl with a shiny ribbon making laser noises with pizza. If the College of Sciences had a recruiting poster, this should be the billboard.
Maya: (deadpan) Absolutely not. We smell like Crazy Bread, 87-octane fuel, and untreated sleep deprivation. They'd revoke the university's accreditation on the spot.
Nora: (laughing softly, smiling warmly between the two of them) It's not a perfect week. Neither is the semester. Turns out that's survivable too, when you're not trying to solo the whole syllabus.
Narrator: For forty-five minutes, nobody opens a textbook. Nobody checks Canvas. Jules eats two slices of pizza and half the basket of fries, realizing halfway through that it is the first hot meal he has tasted rather than inhaled over a keyboard in ten days.
Narrator: [BG: The_Mill_Patio_Dusk.jpg] [SPRITE: Jules_Smiling, left] [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, middle] [SPRITE: Maya_Thinking, right] At 6:35 p.m., Maya's phone buzzes against the metal table. It's Jordan Reyes asking if she's still willing to meet at the dairy barn whiteboard before tomorrow morning's 9:00 a.m. Horizon Fellowship panel with Dr. Hale.
Narrator: Maya exhales a long breath, brushing a crumb off her jeans and zipping up her heavy canvas coat as the Thursday evening mist thickens beyond the patio awning.
Maya_Speaking(right): Duty calls in the pasture. Wish me luck in the lion's den tomorrow morning at nine. Dr. Hale is sharpening his red pen as we speak.
Jules_Speaking(left): You don't need luck, Sterling. You already know that barn better than Hale knows his own footnotes. Just don't let him make you build your case twice.
Nora_Hoodie(middle): And don't let a ghost in a tweed jacket evict you from a house you built. Knock 'em dead, Maya.
Narrator: [HIDE: right] Maya grins, gives them a two-finger salute, and heads out into the mist, leaving Jules and Nora in the warm amber glow of the patio string lights as the fourth night of the week finally settles into peace.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scene 6: Friday Morning - The Fork in the Road
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Narrator: [Scene 6: Friday Morning - The Fork in the Road] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Jules_Dorm_Morning.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, 10:00 a.m. The post-rain Friday morning sunlight cuts cleanly through the open blinds, illuminating dust motes dancing in the warm air.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The bright chirp of birds in the quad oaks outside, mingled with the distant chime of the ten o'clock campus bell.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Thinking, middle] Friday morning arrives, washed clean by Wednesday and Thursday's storms. For the first time all semester, Jules woke up at 8:30 a.m. *before* his alarm went off, having slept seven uninterrupted hours without a single dream about hexadecimal offsets or falling GPAs.
Narrator: At 8:45 a.m., right before Maya walked into her Horizon Fellowship panel with Dr. Vasquez, Dr. Chen, and Dr. Hale, Jules had sent her a single text to steady her nerves: *You already know the barn better than Hale does. Go prove it.*
Narrator: [BG: Campus_Quad_Sunny.jpg] [HIDE: middle] [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, left] [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, right] Now, standing by his third-floor dorm window with a mug of actual hot coffee in his hand, he looks down toward the campus quad.
Narrator: Near the center oak benches, Nora sits in a patch of morning sunlight, lowering her phone from her ear with the quiet, unmistakable relief of someone who just set down a fifty-pound rucksack after carrying it three miles too far.
Narrator: Across the wet green grass, the heavy double doors of the Agricultural Sciences building swing open. Maya steps out in her borrowed navy blazer, unbuttons the collar, and tilts her face up to the crisp post-rain sky, her shoulders completely unknotted.
Narrator: [BG: Jules_Dorm_Morning.jpg] [HIDE: all] [SPRITE: Jules_Thinking, middle] Jules turns back toward his desk. The week's toll is tallied, but the final architectural decision is still his to make.
Narrator: On his whiteboard, the words *RTX -- DISCUSSION SECTION, FINAL PASS* have been half-smudged by his sleeve. On his desk sits his phone, due for his Friday check-in call with his father. Beside it sits his laptop, where the C+ in Stochastic Processes has finally posted to his midterm grade report, nudging his cumulative GPA from a 3.92 to a **3.88**.
Narrator: Will he let the people in his life see the real human behind the numbers, or seal the fortress doors and carry the crushing weight alone?

? Narrator: How does Jules face Friday morning? Does he let them in, or carry it alone one more time?
    > Let them in. (Branch A: Dropping the Guard -- Reach out, plan off-campus, tell Dad the truth)
        -> branch_a
    > Carry it alone. (Branch B: Complete Burnout -- Retreat behind the fortress, protect the illusion)
        -> branch_b

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branch A: Dropping the Guard
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

:: branch_a
Narrator: [Scene 6A: Branch A - Dropping the Guard] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Jules_Dorm_Morning.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, 10:30 a.m. Warm morning light fills the desk corner. The portable monitors are folded neatly into their sleeves.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The upbeat, warm strumming of an acoustic guitar, accompanied by the light clatter of a dry-erase board being wiped clean.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Smiling, middle] Jules picks up the felt eraser and wipes the entire whiteboard clean in three long, sweeping strokes. *RTX -- DISCUSSION SECTION* vanishes into gray dust.
Narrator: He sits down at his desk, opens his laptop, and pulls up a blank Google Doc. Instead of an eighth rewrite of Section 4.2 or a frantic extra-credit plea to his Stochastic Processes professor, he puts his organizational instincts to work on something completely different.
Narrator: At the top of the document, in bold 16-point font, he types:\n**PROJECT: SATURDAY OFF-CAMPUS (REV 1.0 - NO COMPILERS ALLOWED)**
Narrator: Below it, he builds a color-coded table -- using the exact same highlighting palette as his shoebox of flashcards, repurposed for human beings:\n*09:00 AM [GREEN]: El Mirador Trailhead -- 4-mile loop, actual trees, zero fluorescent lighting.*\n*12:30 PM [YELLOW]: La Estrella Diner on Route 4 -- pancakes, hashbrowns, and nobody is allowed to mention cows, CRISPR, or C++.*\n*03:00 PM [BLUE]: Nap / Do Literally Nothing.*
Narrator: And at the bottom of the page, he adds a single footer note: *Nothing circled in red.*
Jules_Smiling(middle): (V.O.) Turns out I contain multitudes outside of embedded systems telemetry. Who knew the color-coding instinct had non-destructive applications?
Narrator: He creates a new group chat with Maya and Nora, names it **The Airlock Survivors**, and drops the link inside.\nWithin twenty seconds, Maya replies: *Finally. Please save me from bovine genetics. Also I justbodied Hale's trick question in the interview, buying first round of coffee right now.*\nNora replies ten seconds later: *On my way to The Mill patio now. Bring your spreadsheets, Rivera. I'm free.*
Narrator: Jules grabs his jacket -- where the blank white flashcard from Tuesday night still rests in his breast pocket -- and heads out the door to meet them in the sun.
Narrator: [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Fire_Escape_Sunset.jpg] [BACKGROUND: The iron fire-escape landing outside Jules's residence hall, Friday, 6:30 p.m. The autumn sky is turning a brilliant, bruised gradient of tangerine and violet over the town rooftops.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The distant hum of Friday evening traffic, coquí-like evening crickets starting up in the damp hedges, and the gentle gust of wind through the iron railings.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, middle] That evening, after spending four hours laughing in the sun at The Mill and helping Maya return her borrowed blazer, Jules sits on the top iron step of the exterior landing with his back against the warm brick wall.
Narrator: His phone is in his hand. He doesn't send a quick text to dodge the conversation. He dials his parents' house number directly, because some things in life demand a human voice.
Jules_Speaking(middle): (as the line picks up on the third ring) Hey, Ma, bendición... Todo bien, sí. Is Pa around too? Put it on speaker if you guys are in the kitchen. I want to tell you both something before the weekend starts.
Narrator: Footsteps shuffle on tile through the speaker, followed by his father's booming, cheerful voice picking up the extension.
Dad: ¡Qué hubo, mi ingeniero! Ready for the big Monday showdown with RTX? I was just telling your mother--
Jules_Speaking(middle): Pa. Wait a second. Let me talk first, please?
Narrator: His father pauses, sensing the unusual gravity in Julian's tone.
Dad: ...Claro, mijo. ¿Qué pasó? Are you sick?
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Distressed, middle] Jules looks out over the orange horizon, takes a deep, four-second breath just like Maya taught him in the booth, and opens the airlock.
Narrator: He tells them everything. He doesn't use engineering jargon to hide behind. He tells them about rewriting the capstone paper six times out of pure, paralyzing terror. He tells them about the `firmware_rev_9` crash on Wednesday night at The Mill, spilling espresso over his notes while his hands shook too hard to type, and submitting the paper four hours late.
Narrator: And then he tells them the hardest part: about the 77% C+ on his Stochastic Processes midterm, and how when the grade posted today, his cumulative GPA officially dropped from a 3.92 to a 3.88.
Jules_Distressed(middle): (his voice trembling, thick with four years of held breath) I'm sorry, Pa. I know you gave up your bonuses for that first Dell laptop, and I know you told Tío Beto and everyone at the plant that I never drop below a three point nine. I haven't been sleeping more than three hours a night for three weeks trying to keep that number alive, and I just... I couldn't hold it up anymore. I was so scared of letting you down.
Narrator: There is a long, deafening silence on the line. Only the faint hum of his mother's kitchen refrigerator comes through the speaker.
Narrator: It stretches for five seconds. Six. Long enough that Jules's stomach drops into cold freefall, his knuckles tightening on the iron railing as hot, pent-up tears finally well over his lower lashes.
Narrator: Then, his father speaks. His voice is quieter than Jules has ever heard it in twenty-one years -- completely stripped of neighborhood bravado, sounding simply like a father sitting at a kitchen table.
Dad: ...Julian. Mijo, escúchame bien.
Jules_Distressed(middle): (whispering) Estoy aquí, Pa.
Dad: That three point nine was never supposed to cost you your soul, Julian. Do you hear me? When I worked those double shifts at the plant, I didn't do it to buy a number on a piece of paper. I did it because I wanted my son to have a life where he didn't have to break his back like I did.
Mom: (her voice chiming in softly from the background, thick with emotion) Te lo dije, viejo... Mi amor, why didn't you tell us you weren't sleeping?
Dad: Your mom has told me a hundred times that studying what you study in that college isn't easy, and I won't pretend I understand half the words on your computer screen. Maybe I brag too much to Beto because I'm proud of you, and that's my fault if I made you feel like you couldn't make a mistake.
Dad: But we love *you*, mijo. Julian. Not the number. Not the company. If RTX takes you with a 3.88, que Dios los bendiga. And if they don't, you come home, we eat arroz con habichuelas, and we figure out the next interview together. ¿Me oíste?
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Smiling, middle] Inside Jules's chest, something that has been wound to the breaking point since freshman orientation finally snaps clean -- not breaking, just releasing. A hot tear slips down his cheek, immediately followed by a shaky, breathless, genuine laugh.
Jules_Smiling(middle): (wiping his cheek with the back of his wrist) Sí, Pa. Te oí. I know. I'm finally starting to know that now.
Dad: Good. Now go eat something that doesn't come out of a plastic cup, and don't touch that computer until Sunday afternoon. That's an order from the house.
Jules_Smiling(middle): Yes, sir. Te quiero, Pa. Love you, Ma.
Narrator: He hangs up the phone and rests his head back against the warm brick, watching the first evening stars prick through the violet sky.
Narrator: He doesn't magically fix the whole semester that night. The 3.88 still sits on his official portal. The RTX Systems Architecture interview still looms at 10:00 a.m. on Monday morning, butterflies and all.
Narrator: But for the first time in four years, Monday morning isn't a guillotine blade hanging between him and being allowed to exist. Tomorrow at nine, there is a muddy trailhead, a diner booth with terrible pancakes, and two friends who already saw the worst crash of his week and pulled up a chair anyway.
System: [FINAL STATS: Vulnerability MAXED | Health & Boundaries HIGH | Burnout RESOLVED]
Narrator: [HIDE: all] "The weight didn't disappear. It never really does... It just stopped being only his to carry."
System: *Ending A: Dropping the Guard.*

