:: branch_b
Narrator: [Scene 6B: Branch B - Complete Burnout] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Jules_Dorm_Night.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, 11:00 a.m. Jules reaches out and pulls the vinyl blinds tightly shut, plunging the room back into a cold, artificial twilight lit only by the blue glare of his LEDs.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The sharp, metallic snap of closing blinds, followed by the relentless, droning hum of cooling fans.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Stressed, middle] On the edge of his desk, Jules's phone buzzes against the wood. The screen lights up with a text from Maya:\n*Maya: Survived Hale!! Coffee or pizza at The Mill later? Nora's in. Come celebrate being alive.*
Narrator: Jules stares at the glowing notification for ten seconds. A part of him -- the part that laughed over curly fries yesterday afternoon, the part that still has a blank white index card in his coat pocket -- reaches toward the screen.
Narrator: Then his eyes flick up to the browser tab on his left monitor: **Cumulative GPA: 3.88**.
Narrator: If he writes an unprompted 15-page supplemental derivation package for his Stochastic Processes professor before Monday morning and begs for a grade reconsideration on Question 2, he can mathematically claw back the four hundredths of a point before the registrar locks midterm standings.
Narrator: Jules picks up his phone, types a single thumbs-up emoji to Maya, hits send, and immediately flips the toggle switch to *Do Not Disturb*.
Narrator: He opens his capstone discussion section document for an eighth rewrite that Dr. Okoye explicitly told him not to do. He logs back into his C++ IDE, opens the memory profiler, and chases the two non-critical heap warnings down to zero. Then he cleans the build cache and compiles it all over again, just to watch the numbers scroll.
Jules_Tired(middle): (V.O.) I don't have room for a diner and a trailhead. Sympathy and pancakes are for people who didn't drop two hundredths of a point before the biggest Monday of their lives. I have room for the 3.9 and the RTX offer. That's the whole equation. That's always been the whole equation.
Narrator: [BG: Jules_Dorm_Night.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, Friday, 9:30 p.m. The room is pitch black except for the blinding, clinical blue glare of three monitors reflecting off his hollow face.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The loud, lonely buzz of a whitelisted phone call vibrating against bare particleboard.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Distressed, middle] At 9:30 p.m., his father's contact bypasses the Do Not Disturb filter. Jules clears his dry throat three times, sits up straight in his ergonomic chair, and answers on the second ring.
Dad: (delighted, shouting warmly over the Friday night music from the neighborhood) ¡Tres punto nueve, Julian! Friday night! RTX on Monday morning! I'm telling everyone at the plant on Monday that my son's already got one foot in the door! How are you feeling, campeón?
Narrator: Jules looks at the 3.88 glowing on his second screen. He looks at the half-written, frantic email begging his math professor for extra credit.
Jules_Distressed(middle): (his voice completely flat, hollow, and robotic, reciting the script from memory) Yeah, Pa. Three point nine holds. Everything's compiled and ready for Monday.
Dad: ¡Ese es mi hijo! Unstoppable, like a machine! Get some rest this weekend, okay? Your mother says bendición!
Jules_Distressed(middle): Bendición, Pa. Talk to you Monday.
Narrator: The line goes dead. Jules slowly lowers the phone to the desk.
Narrator: He doesn't mention that he hasn't eaten a real meal since Thursday's fries. He doesn't mention the three missed texts from Maya and Nora sitting muted in his notification tray, or the Adidas shoebox of color-coded flashcards that he accidentally knocked onto the floor at 7:00 p.m. and didn't have the energy to pick up.
Narrator: By every external metric known to his parents, his department, and his resume, the firewall held. The capstone is in. The extra-credit packet is compiling. The RTX interview is seventy-two hours away.
Narrator: Around 2:00 a.m., he falls asleep sitting upright in his desk chair, his chest vibrating faintly with heart palpitations from five canned espressos, his phone clutched face-up in his cold hand in case the RTX recruiter emails early.
Narrator: [HIDE: all] The dorm room is dead quiet. The pixel-art rain loops endlessly across his monitors. He is flawless, he is prepared, and he is entirely, precisely alone. Like he always thought he had to be.
System: [FINAL STATS: Burnout CRITICAL | Vulnerability ZERO | Health DEPLETED]
Narrator: "The weight was still there. It was still only his to bear, and his alone. He didn't want to be a burden. Now he knows the price of it..."
System: *Ending B: Complete Burnout.*